                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ea6e4101-3ea3-3a9d-99cf-d6e8103b03d7",
                        "entry_id": "06160cae-ebee-3d2c-bf1a-b371fe68caf1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To boil or seethe."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4d17e0be-1575-4766-9145-4abddd263e30",
                                "sense_id": "ea6e4101-3ea3-3a9d-99cf-d6e8103b03d7",
                                "text": "The still liquid element startled uprears, It bubbled and bullered and roared in his ears, Like thunder that gallows on high."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bde8a30f-fcf6-4283-81e1-22cf337e55d7",
                                "sense_id": "ea6e4101-3ea3-3a9d-99cf-d6e8103b03d7",
                                "text": "If this wer a good argument, to prove a man's sins wer not pardoned, because he still finds corruption bullering up in him, then none in the world should have their sin pardoned!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fbcbf2cf-a247-49d5-a895-ad85afab463f",
                                "sense_id": "ea6e4101-3ea3-3a9d-99cf-d6e8103b03d7",
                                "text": "The end of an assay in the Edinburgh mint was described exactly so in 1639: 'in this haill tyme it was always bullering and as it were seithing bot so soone as it settled and rested from so doeing (al the quhich tyme there did appeir some cleir twinkling sparkles into it)' the assayer 'tooke it forthe bot not in ane instant bot softely by retireing and advancing."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
